<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The normalized scores of Reinformer with AG (RF+AG) and other baseline algorithms. Traditional algorithms and DT-based algorithms are separated to the left and right sides. The best scores among all DT-based algorithms are <span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Comparison Experiments with Policy Gradient</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that the mere incorporation of policy gradient techniques into DT-based methods does not lead to a stable improvement in performance. To validate this claim, based on RF, we introduce a policy gradient term to the loss function, drawing upon RF, and assess its efficacy. We examine three distinct methodologies that utilize a critic network: PG, AWAC  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nair et al. (2020)</a></cite>, and AG. To ensure the internal validity of our experiment, the implementation of these three methods adheres to the OAMPI paradigm  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brandfonbrener et al. (2021)</a></cite>, employing critic networks that share identical parameters and architecture. The implementation of PG references the work of QT  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which involves the addition of a normalized policy gradient term to the loss function. The loss function is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\pi}=\mathcal{L}_{DT}-\alpha\frac{\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\tau_{t}\sim\mathcal{D},\\
s_{i}\sim\tau_{t}\end{subarray}}\left[Q(s_{i},\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})\right]}{\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\tau_{t}\sim\mathcal{D},\\
s_{i}\sim\tau_{t}\end{subarray}}\left[|Q(s_{i},\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})|\right]}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\pi}=\mathcal{L}_{DT}-\alpha\frac{\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\tau_{t}\sim\mathcal{D},\\
s_{i}\sim\tau_{t}\end{subarray}}\left[Q(s_{i},\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})\right]}{\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\tau_{t}\sim\mathcal{D},\\
s_{i}\sim\tau_{t}\end{subarray}}\left[|Q(s_{i},\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})|\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The AWAC method is another method that uses a critic in the training phase. The AWR  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al. (2019)</a></cite> method, which is similar to the AWAC method, is used in the ADT  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. (2023)</a></cite> method, and is not chosen for our experiments since the AWR method also involves estimation of the V-value. This method does not add an extra term but instead adds weight to the original loss, which is defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\pi}=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\tau_{t}\sim\mathcal{D},\\
s_{i},a_{i}\sim\tau_{t}\end{subarray}}\bigg[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\pi}=\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\tau_{t}\sim\mathcal{D},\\
s_{i},a_{i}\sim\tau_{t}\end{subarray}}\bigg[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{log}p(a_{i}|\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>log</mtext><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{log}p(a_{i}|\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{exp}(\frac{1}{\lambda}(Q(s_{i},a_{i})-Q(s_{i},\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})))\bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{exp}(\frac{1}{\lambda}(Q(s_{i},a_{i})-Q(s_{i},\pi(\tau_{t})_{i})))\bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is noticeable that this loss involves the NLL loss term rather than the MSE loss term. This approach diverges from the original DT  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a></cite> but aligns with several improved algorithms  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Given that our baseline algorithm is RF, no modifications to the implementation are necessary, and the entropy loss term is computed independently. Except for the loss function (AG is not modified, but it modifies the evaluation step), the hyperparameters are kept the same, and the results are displayed in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Comparison Experiments with Policy Gradient ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Adjusting the Output of Decision Transformer with Action Gradient" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. It is reasonable that, in a majority of environments, the incorporation of a critic network, regardless of the method employed, improves the performance of the algorithm. As discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Extrapolation Ability ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Adjusting the Output of Decision Transformer with Action Gradient" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the ability for state-level extrapolation is augmented by these methods. On the other hand, from AWAC’s theory, moves with high Q in the trajectory are given higher weights, and PG would have a similar effect.</p>
</div>
